<!-- SECTION 15A: LEGAL FRAMEWORK -->
<section class="content-section">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
            <h2 class="section-title">Legal Framework for Musical Work Copyright</h2>
            <div class="content-text">
                
                <p><strong>Section 2(p):</strong> Musical work = work consisting of music + notation, excluding words/actions. Source: <a href="https://www.copyright.gov.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">copyright.gov.in</a></p><p><strong>Section 2(d)(ii):</strong> Composer is the author of a musical work.</p><p><strong>Section 14(a):</strong> Exclusive rights - reproduce, issue copies, perform in public, communicate, adapt (arrange/transcribe), translate.</p><p><strong>Section 22:</strong> Duration - composer's lifetime + 60 years.</p><p><strong>Section 18(1) Proviso (2012 Amendment):</strong> Non-waivable right to royalties for composers whose works are in sound recordings (not cinematograph films). Any contrary assignment is void.</p><p><strong>Section 33 / IPRS:</strong> IPRS is the government-registered copyright society. Re-registered 21 Jan 2025. Source: <a href="https://iprs.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">iprs.org</a></p><p><strong>Section 31C:</strong> Cover version statutory licence after 5 years from first recording with prescribed royalties.</p><p><strong>Penalties:</strong> Section 63: 6 months to 3 years + Rs 50,000-2 lakh. Section 55: injunction, damages. Section 57: moral rights persist.</p>

            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- FAQ SECTION -->
<section id="faq-section" class="content-section" style="background-color: #ffffff;">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
            <div class="faq-expanded">
                <aside class="faq-expanded__aside">
                    <h2 class="faq-expanded__title">Frequently Asked Questions - Musical Work Copyright in Pune</h2>
                    <p class="faq-expanded__lead">Answers about Section 2(p) definition, registration, musical work vs sound recording, royalty rights, IPRS, cover versions, and jingle copyright.</p>
                    @include('partials.bigin-form', [
                        'variant'  => 'compact',
                        'service'  => 'Copyright for Musical Work in Pune',
                        'city'     => 'Pune',
                    ])
                </aside>
                <div class="faq-expanded__list">
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-1">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is a musical work under the Copyright Act?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Under Section 2(p), a musical work means a work consisting of music including graphical notation, but excluding words or actions intended to be sung, spoken, or performed with the music. The composition - melody, harmony, arrangement - is the musical work. Lyrics are a separate literary work. Sound recording is yet another separate copyrightable work.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-2">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">How to register music copyright in Pune?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Prepare the composition (score/notation and/or audio), file Form XIV on copyright.gov.in with Rs 500 (individual) or Rs 2,000 (company), wait 30 days mandatory objection period, respond to examination queries, receive certificate in approximately 6 months. Entirely online. Patron handles complete process from Pune.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-3">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is the difference between musical work and sound recording copyright?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Musical work copyright (Section 2(p)) protects the composition - melody, harmony, arrangement - owned by the composer. Sound recording copyright (Section 2(xx)) protects the recorded version owned by the producer. Two separate copyrights coexist. Both can be registered separately on copyright.gov.in.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-4">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Does a composer retain royalty rights after assignment?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes. The 2012 Amendment proviso to Section 18(1) makes the right to receive royalties non-waivable for composers and lyricists (for sound recordings not part of cinematograph films). Any assignment clause waiving this right is void. Royalties collected through IPRS.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-5">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">How long does music copyright last in India?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Copyright in a musical work lasts for the composer's lifetime plus 60 years from the beginning of the calendar year following the year of death (Section 22). Sound recording copyright lasts 60 years from publication. Posthumous works: 60 years from first publication.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-6">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is IPRS and how to join?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>IPRS (Indian Performing Right Society) is the government-authorised copyright society under Section 33 for musical and literary works. Collects and distributes royalties for public performance, broadcasting, and digital use. Composers with at least one published work can apply at iprs.org. Re-registered 21 January 2025.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-7">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is a cover version under Section 31C?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Section 31C provides a statutory licence allowing any person to make a cover version of a musical work already recorded, after the existing recordings have been in circulation for 5 years. Must pay prescribed royalties and comply with conditions including advance notice and no alteration to the basic character.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-8">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Can a jingle or brand soundtrack be copyrighted?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes. A jingle or brand soundtrack is a musical work under Section 2(p) eligible for copyright registration. The composer is the author and first copyright owner. When commissioned, a written assignment (Section 18) is needed to transfer ownership. Patron drafts these deeds for Pune agencies and brands.</p></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>


            <!-- Quick Answers -->
            <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:32px;">
                <p><strong>Quick Answers</strong></p>
                <p><strong>Music ka copyright kaise milta hai Pune mein?</strong> copyright.gov.in pe Form XIV file karo. Rs 500 (individual). Score ya audio submit karo. 30 din wait. ~6 mahine certificate. Patron Pune se handle karta hai.</p><p><strong>Composer ko royalty milti rehti hai kya assignment ke baad bhi?</strong> Haan! 2012 Amendment ke baad Section 18 proviso - composer ka royalty right waive nahi ho sakta. IPRS se collect hoti hai.</p><p><strong>Musical work aur sound recording mein kya fark hai?</strong> Musical work = composition (melody, harmony). Sound recording = studio record. Dono ka alag copyright. Composer ka music pe, producer ka recording pe.</p>

            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

